---
/**
 * Schools hub — /for-schools/
 *
 * A calm, text-led routing page for school staff: three sections that
 * separate curriculum/policy review from classroom delivery, and a short
 * closing section for the times reading isn't enough. It links fourteen
 * teacher articles and five service pages and says nothing they don't.
 *
 * Deliberately plain: no cards, no photography, no downloads, no prices,
 * no testimonials, no pupil-layer (Okay to Ask) links. The linked articles
 * carry the statutory detail and its citations; this page makes no legal,
 * medical or effectiveness claim of its own.
 *
 * Service links are plain routes with no ?service= parameter — the service
 * pages own that mapping through CtaServiceEnquiry.
 *
 * Search: both top-level regions carry `data-pagefind-body`. Every other
 * indexed template marks a body, so a page without one is dropped from the
 * index entirely — that is why this hub was missing from the generated
 * index rather than ranking badly. The hero is marked alongside the section
 * wrapper so the H1 and the lede are indexed with the section prose. The H1
 * also sets `title` explicitly rather than relying on Pagefind's automatic
 * first-H1 rule, so the result title stays "For schools" regardless of how
 * the body regions are later rearranged.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { breadcrumbJsonLd } from '../lib/site';

const description =
  'Guidance for school staff reviewing RSE: curriculum and policy, teaching the hardest topics in the classroom, and the support available when your school wants help with either.';

const jsonLd = breadcrumbJsonLd([{ name: 'For schools', path: '/for-schools/' }]);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="RSE guidance and teaching support for schools | Tailor RSE"
  description={description}
  canonicalPath="/for-schools/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <header class="schools-hub-hero" data-pagefind-body>
    <div class="schools-hub-hero__inner">
      <div class="schools-hub-hero__text">
        <h1 class="schools-hub-hero__title" data-pagefind-meta="title">For schools</h1>
        <p class="schools-hub-hero__lede">
          If your school is reviewing RSE this year, the work tends to arrive
          in two halves. One half is the policy, the curriculum map and the
          letter to parents. The other half is preparing the teacher who will
          lead a difficult conversation in the classroom. The writing collected
          here is for school staff working on either half.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="schools-hub" data-pagefind-body>
    <section class="schools-hub-section" aria-labelledby="schools-hub-curriculum">
      <div class="schools-hub-section__inner">
        <h2 id="schools-hub-curriculum" class="schools-hub-section__title">
          Reviewing your curriculum and policy
        </h2>
        <p>
          Start with
          <a class="link-inline" href="/blog/2026-rse-guidance-plain-english-summary/">What the 2026 RSE guidance means for your school now</a>
          if you are putting your curriculum map next to the revised DfE
          guidance and trying to work out which gaps matter first. It starts
          with the lessons pupils are about to receive, names the sections worth
          reading closely, and works back to the policy and the information
          families have been given.
        </p>
        <p>
          For a phase-specific starting point, read the
          <a class="link-inline" href="/blog/2026-rse-guidance-primary-schools/">primary RSE guidance</a>
          or the <a class="link-inline" href="/blog/2026-rse-guidance-secondary-schools/">secondary RSE guidance</a>.
          The <a class="link-inline" href="/blog/rse-policy-checklist-2026/">RSE policy checklist</a>
          and <a class="link-inline" href="/blog/rse-policy-questions-governors-ask/">questions for governors</a>
          can help organise the policy review and the discussion that follows.
        </p>
        <p>
          For work with families, start with
          <a class="link-inline" href="/blog/consulting-parents-rse-policy-2026/">consulting parents on your RSE policy</a>
          and the <a class="link-inline" href="/blog/parental-right-to-withdraw-rse-2026/">parental right to withdraw</a>.
          <a class="link-inline" href="/blog/reduce-withdrawals-sex-education-parent-consultation/">Reducing withdrawals through parent consultation</a>
          looks at the conversations and classroom activities that can help parents understand the teaching.
        </p>
        <p>
          Primary leads planning the puberty sequence will find the decisions
          set out in
          <a class="link-inline" href="/blog/teaching-puberty-primary-schools-guide/">Teaching Puberty Before It Happens: A Guide for Primary Schools</a>,
          including when to teach it, why every pupil learns about menstruation,
          and how to handle the questions that follow a parent letter. In
          special schools the planning starts from a different place, and
          <a class="link-inline" href="/blog/complete-guide-rse-special-schools/">A Complete Guide to Developing RSE in Special Schools</a>
          works through policy, curriculum design, staff confidence and parent
          engagement for pupils with complex needs.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="schools-hub-section" aria-labelledby="schools-hub-classroom">
      <div class="schools-hub-section__inner">
        <h2 id="schools-hub-classroom" class="schools-hub-section__title">
          Teaching the topics that are hardest to get right
        </h2>
        <p>
          If you teach RSE alongside another subject,
          <a class="link-inline" href="/blog/teaching-hard-topics-when-not-a-specialist/">Teaching the hard topics when you're not a specialist</a>
          is about what happens in the room when the topic is uncomfortable and
          you are the adult holding it: preparing the moments you are dreading,
          what to do with a question you weren't expecting, and who to turn to
          when a topic is too much to carry alone.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a class="link-inline" href="/blog/talking-to-boys-masculinity-misogyny-online-influencers/">Talking to Boys About Masculinity, Misogyny and Online Influencers</a>
          is about the class where someone quotes an influencer and the room
          turns to watch you.
          <a class="link-inline" href="/blog/teaching-consent-beyond-no-means-no/">How to Teach Consent Beyond &ldquo;No Means No&rdquo;</a>
          goes past the legal definition into the grey areas pupils raise.
          <a class="link-inline" href="/blog/sharing-nudes-sextortion-deepfakes-schools-2026/">Sharing Nudes, Sextortion and Deepfakes: What Schools Need to Know in 2026</a>
          covers what has changed online and how a lesson can handle blame and
          reporting alongside your safeguarding arrangements.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="schools-hub-section" aria-labelledby="schools-hub-support">
      <div class="schools-hub-section__inner">
        <h2 id="schools-hub-support" class="schools-hub-section__title">
          Bringing someone in
        </h2>
        <p>
          Reading is sometimes enough. When you need help making the policy and
          the teaching describe the same programme,
          <a class="link-inline" href="/services/rse-policy-curriculum-planning/">RSE policy and curriculum planning</a>
          gives you support with both. Teaching teams can use
          <a class="link-inline" href="/services/rse-training/">RSE training</a>
          to prepare for the work in the classroom.
        </p>
        <p>
          For direct delivery, a
          <a class="link-inline" href="/services/drop-days/">drop day</a>
          puts a Tailor educator in front of your year group for the day.
          Whole-school SEND placements have a separate
          <a class="link-inline" href="/services/rse-for-special-schools/">RSE in special schools</a>
          offer, built for pupils on EHCPs.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you are not sure which of those fits, the
          <a class="link-inline" href="/services/">services overview</a>
          sets out the full range, including primary, secondary and alternative
          provision. Start with a conversation about where your school is now,
          and we will work out what would help most.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Hero — text-only two-tier wrapper (shell alignment, hero-text measure) */
  /* --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .schools-hub-hero {
    width: 100%;
    background: var(--bg-tinted);
    border-bottom: var(--border-width-xs) solid var(--border-subtle);
    padding: var(--space-struct-y-base) var(--space-global-gutter);
  }

  .schools-hub-hero__inner {
    margin-inline: auto;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: var(--container-max-shell);
  }

  .schools-hub-hero__text {
    max-width: var(--container-max-hero-text);
  }

  .schools-hub-hero__title {
    font-family: var(--font-tailor-heading-stack);
    font-size: var(--text-display-size-h1);
    font-weight: var(--heading-weight-h1);
    line-height: var(--lh-display);
    color: var(--text-heading);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-global-md);
  }

  .schools-hub-hero__lede {
    font-family: var(--font-tailor-body-stack);
    font-size: var(--text-prose-size-lede);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-regular);
    line-height: var(--lh-prose);
    color: var(--text-body);
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Sections — long-form reading measure, one shared typography rule       */
  /* --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .schools-hub {
    background: var(--bg-page);
  }

  .schools-hub-section {
    width: 100%;
  }

  .schools-hub-section__inner {
    margin-inline: auto;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: var(--container-max-prose);
    padding: var(--space-struct-y-base) var(--space-global-gutter) 0;
  }

  .schools-hub-section:last-child .schools-hub-section__inner {
    padding-bottom: var(--space-struct-y-base);
  }

  .schools-hub-section__title {
    font-family: var(--font-tailor-heading-stack);
    font-size: var(--text-prose-size-h2);
    font-weight: var(--heading-weight-h2);
    line-height: var(--lh-heading);
    color: var(--text-heading);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-global-sm);
  }

  .schools-hub-section p {
    font-family: var(--font-tailor-body-stack);
    font-size: var(--text-prose-size-body);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-regular);
    line-height: var(--lh-prose);
    color: var(--text-body);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-global-sm);
  }

  .schools-hub-section p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
</style>
